{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011Cf84Zxy8JkyJTUXQh5dxW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# How a Bicycle Rim Brake Works

When the rider squeezes the brake lever, they pull a cable that runs from the handlebar down to the brake mechanism. This cable is under tension and transmits the pulling force directly to the brake arms, causing them to move inward toward the wheel.

The brake pads, which are attached to the ends of the brake arms, press against both sides of the rim as the arms move together. The friction between the pads and the rim surface slows the wheel's rotation, bringing the bicycle to a stop. Releasing the lever allows a spring to push the arms back apart, separating the pads from the rim."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":64,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":144,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}}

